 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Nov 19, 2026 
 
 
 Today, we look at the macroeconomic — and political economy — implications of a major permitting reform bill that crossed a key procedural hurdle in the U.S. Senate this week.

 Plus, what the Federal Reserve minutes tell us about the (near-certain) rate hike on the way next month. Today's newsletter, edited by George Moriarty and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 950 words, a 3.5-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Permitting reform shows a pivot to the supply side 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 You can't solve a supply-side problem with a demand-side solution. This is the year that a bipartisan majority of Congress came to grips with that reality.

 The big picture: The U.S. economy has been held back this decade by inadequate supplies of housing, energy and infrastructure. There's now surprising momentum on Capitol Hill to change that.

 Driving the news: The Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act, which reduces permitting burdens for a wide range of projects, passed a key procedural step in the Senate by a 65-31 vote yesterday.

 It follows an overwhelming bipartisan passage of the Road to Housing Act earlier this year, which aims to make it easier to build homes. Zoom in: The permitting reform legislation reflects a delicate legislative balancing act.

 Most Republicans in the Senate supported it as a regulation-reducing action that will ease the way for more energy infrastructure. It has divided Democrats and environmental groups between those who see urgency in reducing red tape to enable more green energy infrastructure and those more inclined to defend the thicket of environmental and other protections that can bog projects down for years. Its prospects in the House — and thus to become law in the current lame-duck Congressional session — remain uncertain. Between the lines: It's taken a long time, but the political system has gradually come to the recognition that the central problems of the U.S. economy in the 2020s boil down to inadequate supply.

 That's in contrast with the post-financial crisis economy of the 2010s, when the predominant problem was inadequate demand. Back then, interest rates and inflation were persistently too low, unemployment too high, and stimulus came with little obvious cost. Now, the U.S. faces the opposite dynamics — a reality Congress and the public are starting to catch up to. Context: A letter supporting the permitting reform legislation , for example, was signed by a long list of former top officials, including Treasury secretaries from the last four administrations (Janet Yellen from President Biden, Steve Mnuchin from President Trump, Tim Geithner from President Obama, and Hank Paulson from President George W. Bush).

 What they're saying: "We cannot build if we strangle ourselves with red tape," said Gina Raimondo, Biden's Commerce secretary, at a Fed conference this week. You want the prices of housing and manufacturing to go down, then you have to shrink the time."

 She adds that "these regulations are there for good purpose, but somewhere along the way we got overtorqued because if it takes seven years to put a shovel in the ground, that's a problem and that's expensive and that's bad for America and stands in the way of productivity." Reality check: The political impulse to address affordability through demand-side measures — stimulus that pumps money into people's pockets — remains alive and well in some quarters.

 Trump promised $5,000 checks to American families if Republicans held Congress. Rep. Ro Khanna, a progressive who may run for president, this week called for $1,700 payments to American households to offset higher costs due to the Iran war. Yes, but: Trump's checks idea only gathered 43%, per an Economist/YouGov poll , and obviously did not prevent a Republican wipeout in midterm elections.

 The bottom line: Congress is at least starting to grapple with America's supply-constrained economy, even if the focus is not universal.

 
 
